retain-model-payloads-in-native-traces

Lane: cross

Unit: retain-model-diagnostics-for-thirty-six-hours

ChildPatch: retain-model-payloads-in-native-traces

Form: GitDiff

Segment: schema

Purpose: Retain complete local model transport diagnostics and typed response-attempt facts for the same rolling thirty-six-hour window in the native logs database.

Requires: none

Provides: Trace outbound and inbound WebSocket payloads and admit HTTP, SSE and WebSocket timing payloads to the SQLite log sink. Define shared response facts and store typed attempts in a separate responses table in logs_2.sqlite through native migrations and the existing StateRuntime logs pool. Supply filtered cursor pages, model membership and compact generation restoration with revision and outcome facts for recent-24H success counts, including unfinished attempts, and latest-ended-generation timing. Prune logs and responses older than 36 hours through existing startup and periodic maintenance without active size or row caps; keep feedback exports capped at 10 MiB.

Boundary: Ordinary log queueing, failure reporting, recursive-runtime suppression, native SQLite connection and recovery policy, transport behavior, feedback export ordering and unrelated noisy-target filters remain native. Preserve native cap algorithms and tests behind disabled local retention policy. Structured response persistence does not use the lossy tracing queue or open an additional pool or database file. StateRuntime owns shared pool closure. Request observation, ordered writing and drain, live publication, in-memory history, metric and success-rate calculation, App Server integration and TUI presentation remain in their respective Units. Preserve conversation history, full-history Usage accounting and unrelated databases. No legacy response database reader or automatic old-file cleanup is retained.

Verification: State log filter and feedback tests preserve payload admission and bounded ordered exports. Response-store tests cover typed unknown-versus-zero round trips, configured logs database placement, coexistence with ordinary logs, filters, cursors, restart, expiry, compact ongoing and terminal outcome restoration, and shared pool closure with standalone main-file reopening. Logs maintenance tests cover the shared 36-hour boundary, size independence, startup and idle cleanup while native cap tests remain intact.

CrossGroup: control-center


